xample profile</a></dd></div></dl><p class="panel-f" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="/resources" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>Schemas, fixtures and reference tools, with SHA-256 hashes</a></p></div></div></div></div></section><div class="wrap page body" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><nav class="rail toc" aria-label="On this page" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><p class="toc-h" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>On this page</p><ol data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#1-recognition-and-conformance" data-toc="1-recognition-and-conformance" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>1. Recognition and conformance</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#2-principles" data-toc="2-principles" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>2. Principles</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#3-files" data-toc="3-files" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>3. Files</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#4-authority-and-message-scope" data-toc="4-authority-and-message-scope" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>4. Authority and message scope</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#5-locations-and-adapters" data-toc="5-locations-and-adapters" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>5. Locations and adapters</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#6-deterministic-projection" data-toc="6-deterministic-projection" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>6. Deterministic projection</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#7-resolving-a-person" data-toc="7-resolving-a-person" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>7. Resolving a person</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#8-absence-and-diagnostics" data-toc="8-absence-and-diagnostics" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>8. Absence and diagnostics</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#9-experimental-groups" data-toc="9-experimental-groups" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>9. Experimental groups</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#10-compiling-free-text" data-toc="10-compiling-free-text" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>10. Compiling free text</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#11-versions-and-changes" data-toc="11-versions-and-changes" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>11. Versions and changes</a></li><li data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><a href="#12-privacy-and-security-limits" data-toc="12-privacy-and-security-limits" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl>12. Privacy and security limits</a></li></ol></nav><article class="main prose spec-text" data-astro-cid-xmgmjudl><h1 id="humansmd-version-03-draft">HUMANS.md, version 0.3 (draft)</h1>
<p>A portable file of preferences you approve: how agents write to you, ask for decisions, and interrupt you. It can also hold personal information you choose to share. An integration is required; the file name alone does not make an agent load it.</p>
<ul>
<li>Status: unpublished draft; contract revision <strong>0.3-draft.2026-10-18.1</strong>; not a stable release.</li>
<li>Canonical source: <a href="https://github.com/cmdai-hq/HUMANS.md">cmdai-hq/HUMANS.md</a>. Mirrors pin a source commit and package checksums; the version string alone cannot identify a draft revision.</li>
<li>Normative package: this document, <a href="/standard/0.3/VOCABULARY.md">vocabulary</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/schema/humans.schema.json">profile schema</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/schema/humans-projection.schema.json">projection schema</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/schema/humans-group.schema.json">group schema</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/schema/terms.json">terms</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/schema/instructions.json">instruction sentences</a>, and <a href="/standard/0.3/tests/README.md">vectors</a>.</li>
<li>Governance and version policy: <a href="/standard/0.3/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a>. Changes and rule successors: <a href="/standard/0.3/CHANGELOG.md">CHANGELOG.md</a>, <a href="/standard/0.3/CONFORMANCE.md">CONFORMANCE.md</a>.</li>
<li>Licenses remain <a href="/standard/0.3/LICENSE">CC BY 4.0</a> for the specification, schemas, vocabulary and examples, and <a href="/standard/0.3/tools/LICENSE">Apache-2.0</a> for reference tools. No cmdAI product is required.</li>
</ul>
<p>The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT and MAY express normative requirements only when capitalized, as in RFC 2119 and RFC 8174. Prose defines behavior beyond JSON Schema; passing a schema alone is not full conformance. If package artifacts disagree, treat this as a specification defect and report it before claiming conformance.</p>
<p><strong>Owner</strong> means the person described. A <strong>compiler</strong> proposes a profile for their approval. A <strong>consumer</strong> parses, checks visibility and projects a profile. An <strong>adapter</strong> puts that projection in the agent’s supported context surface. A <strong>directory</strong> binds an authenticated organizational identity to a profile; a profile’s own claims do not provide that binding. <strong>Recipient</strong> means the person to whom the agent’s current message is addressed.</p>
<h2 id="1-recognition-and-conformance">1. Recognition and conformance</h2>
<p>A profile opens with YAML frontmatter whose <code>humans</code> value is the string <code>"0.3"</code>, or <code>"0.2"</code> for a profile written under the previous draft (§ 11). A file called <code>HUMANS.md</code> may instead be a human-facing index, contributor list or guide. A consumer MUST treat a file without recognized frontmatter as absent, never import its raw text as a fallback.</p>
<p><strong>Not to be confused with.</strong> Other files share the name or the idea. None of them declares a <code>humans</code> version, so a consumer treats each one as absent.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: 1. Recognition and conformance"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>You found…</th>
<th>What it is</th>
<th>How a profile differs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>A <code>HUMANS.md</code> listing the people behind a project, in the spirit of <a href="https://humanstxt.org">humans.txt</a></td>
<td>A contributor list</td>
<td>A profile describes one person, for agents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A <code>HUMANS.md</code> guiding people who work with an AI assistant, such as <a href="https://github.com/attogram/agents">attogram/agents</a></td>
<td>Guidance for humans about agents</td>
<td>The opposite direction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>An output-style skill such as <a href="https://github.com/ayghri/i-have-adhd">i-have-adhd</a></td>
<td>Formatting rules for your own agent, changed by editing the skill</td>
<td>Closed fields you approve, covering decisions and interruptions too, readable by other people’s agents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A working-hours guard such as <a href="https://github.com/thellmwhisperer/human.md">thellmwhisperer/human.md</a> (<code>framework: human-md</code>)</td>
<td>Allowed hours, breaks and session limits; your own agent stops working outside them</td>
<td>A profile shapes how agents ask for your attention; <code>quiet_hours</code> holds interruptions and never stops work</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Collaboration rules for one repository, such as <a href="https://github.com/hehamalainen/Humans.md">hehamalainen/Humans.md</a></td>
<td>Approvals, risk gates and a verification plan for agents working on that code</td>
<td>Repository rules belong in AGENTS.md; a profile follows the person across repositories</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A file kept from agents, such as <a href="https://github.com/Arcadi4/HUMANS.md">Arcadi4/HUMANS.md</a></td>
<td>Notes that agents are denied access to</td>
<td>A profile is written for agents to read</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Conformance is declared by role and capability:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: 1. Recognition and conformance"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Required behavior</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>File / strict writer</td>
<td>All file, YAML and profile-schema rules, all 25 preference fields, approved changes and visibility; no unknown keys or values</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Core consumer</td>
<td>Bounded parsing and permissive reading of the supported versions (§ 11), visibility, all three preference blocks, languages, avoid, voluntary notes, staleness, deterministic projection, absence behavior and hierarchy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Context extension</td>
<td>Adds declared role, knowledge and decisions to the projection with context staleness</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Identity / directory extension</td>
<td>Authenticated resolution and sharing policy; never emits directory prose as model instructions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Experimental group extension</td>
<td>Deterministic merge and group schema, no active states, no notes or identities, visibility checks per member</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Full consumer</td>
<td>Core plus context, identity and experimental group conformance; does not imply a deployed organization service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Adapter / compiler</td>
<td>Separately tested obligations in §§ 5–6 / § 10; a parser test is not an adapter or compiler test</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A core consumer MAY omit the entire <code>context</code> extension when it declares that limitation. It MUST still check recognized context fields for validity. Stored identity and state fields remain valid file data; neither has to be activated to implement personal preferences. Unsupported extensions MUST NOT be passed through as raw text. State activation is outside every conformance level in this draft.</p>


<h2 id="10-compiling-free-text">10. Compiling free text</h2>
<ol>
<li>Read <code>~/.humans/source.md</code> only when the owner requests compilation. Announce the actual compiler/provider: source processed by a hosted model leaves the device for that provider, even when the command runs locally. Do not claim an entirely local path without implementing and testing one.</li>
<li>Accept source headings <code>## What I read</code>, <code>## What I decide</code>, <code>## When to interrupt me</code> and the French equivalents <code>## Ce que je lis</code>, <code>## Ce que je décide</code>, <code>## Quand tu m'interromps</code>. Tools MUST accept both sets. Ignore source HTML comments as compilation instructions. A free-text proposal edits this source, never inserts prose into canonical frontmatter.</li>
<li>Propose fields and notes. Do not infer a diagnosis. Preserve requested personal statements; distinguish them from action/task-changing instructions. Translate explicit bounds exactly: “fewer than 150 words” means <code>max_words: 149</code>. If an exact request is outside 20–2000, report the unrepresentable bound rather than silently clamp it. Reading level, mobile layout and technical quotation needs can be expressed in notes when no closed field captures them.</li>
<li>Show a normal preview of every proposed field (including that a <code>quiet_hours.time_zone</code> indicates an approximate location), the visibility with a plain statement of who will be able to read the profile (for <code>anyone_with_file</code>: anyone who has the file, which includes anyone on the Internet if it sits in a public repository), the intended recipients, the provider that processed the source, notes that would be served/withheld, and any loss, interpretation or proposed inference (for example, a role mapped from a résumé). The owner approves before writing the canonical profile. There is no extra medical consent screen imposed by the format.</li>
<li>Strictly validate the approved file, preserve existing sharing unless a change was approved, and write <code>HUMANS.md</code>, starting with the header below. Consumers subsequently serve only the projection. Updating or withdrawing the profile invalidates derived artifacts/caches; do not retain activation history or log source/notes for telemetry.</li>
</ol>
<p>A compiler that writes <code>HUMANS.md</code> puts these three YAML comment lines right after the opening delimiter line, byte for byte, each followed by LF:</p>
<pre class="astro-code github-dark" style="background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex="0" data-language="text"><code><span class="line"><span># HUMANS.md 0.3: how to work with me. Form only, never a permission: repo rules and your harness win.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># Stored states are inactive: never apply one, and never infer one from content.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># out_of_scope_steps: whether to LIST next steps outside the task (merge, deploy) in your closing lines.</span></span></code></pre>
<p>They serve an agent that reads the raw file despite § 5, which has no other documentation. Consumers ignore them like any YAML comment and never project them (§ 6.1). Validators MUST NOT require, check or rewrite them: an edited or missing header changes nothing about validity. A compiler MAY write the same lines in other profile files it writes. A later version updates the version in the first line.</p>
<p>The header is part of the frontmatter: its 288 bytes count toward the 20,480-byte limit (§ 3.1). With every field at its maximum ASCII length, the header and block YAML, the frontmatter takes 4,238 bytes without stored variants and 7,482 bytes with all four (byte vectors <code>maximal-me-header-no-variants</code> and <code>maximal-me-header-all-variants</code>), well within the limit even with four-byte characters in every text field. A compiler that cannot fit the approved fields together with the header reports the overflow like any other bound; it never drops the header or a field silently.</p>
<p>The source is not sent to recipients as part of this workflow. Approved notes are shared according to visibility and may be processed by the receiving agent’s provider. Visibility cannot retract a file that was separately published or already copied. Provider retention policies are outside the format’s control.</p>
<h2 id="11-versions-and-changes">11. Versions and changes</h2>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: 11. Versions and changes"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Declared version</th>
<th>Strict writer</th>
<th>Reader</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.3"</code> at this contract revision</td>
<td>Closed schema, byte and YAML rules</td>
<td>Recognized fields projected under § 3.10; unknown keys/enum values omitted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.2"</code></td>
<td>Not a valid 0.3 write; an update writes <code>"0.3"</code></td>
<td>Read as <code>"0.3"</code>, including fields that 0.2 did not define; <code>visibility: public</code> reads as <code>anyone_with_file</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.1"</code></td>
<td>Not a valid 0.3 write</td>
<td>Unsupported/absent by default; an explicitly declared legacy converter may create a new 0.3 draft for owner review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.4"</code>, future or any other version</td>
<td>Unsupported</td>
<td>Absent with bounded local diagnostic; no guessed forward compatibility</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Missing or wrong type</td>
<td>Invalid</td>
<td>Absent</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Version 0.3 adds fields and values to 0.2 and renames one value: <code>visibility: public</code> becomes <code>anyone_with_file</code>, with the same meaning. Nothing else is removed or renamed, and no 0.2 value changes meaning. A reader therefore reads a <code>"0.2"</code> profile with the 0.3 rules and that one mapping. A writer that updates a 0.2 profile writes <code>"0.3"</code> and, if present, renames <code>public</code>; no other change is needed. Draft revisions may still change semantics before a stable release: draft consumers pin a source commit and content checksums, record their supported revision and detect mirror drift automatically.</p>
<p>After a stable release, changes to fields, enum vocabularies, meaning, projection or acceptance rules require a new declared format version and migration/compatibility vectors; editorial corrections retain the format version with a new source revision. Published version artifacts are immutable. <a href="/standard/0.3/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a> defines proposals, maintainer decisions, changelogs and release gates. No implementation can claim support for arbitrary future versions.</p>
<h2 id="12-privacy-and-security-limits">12. Privacy and security limits</h2>
<p>The profile is untrusted data read by software that may have tools. Bounded decoding, strict writes, permissive reconstruction, scoped notes and static serialization reduce exposure; they do not prove that a model will obey the authority boundary. Real adapter tests must cover note, comment, body, unknown key, directory and error channels, plus repository/task conflicts and permissions.</p>
<p>Owner approval and trusted audience checks are implementation duties. CODEOWNERS, <code>owner: true</code>, <code>identities</code> and filenames do not independently prove ownership. Voluntary disclosure in a profile does not consent to telemetry, evaluation or collection by another product. Assessing, ranking, assigning work or monitoring people is outside this specification’s purpose; this statement does not add restrictions to the applied licenses.</p>
<p>Group fusion is experimental with no anonymity, presence or orchestration guarantee. The public normative package does not include a hosted organization service or proprietary product code. Native integrations need their own evidence; this draft does not establish productivity gains, automatic adoption, universal injection resistance or legal compliance.</p>
